## ABSTRACT
Inhibitors that target diabetes pathology-related signaling pathways have great therapeutic potential for diabetic wound healing. Metal–organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  are  increasingly  popular  drug  delivery  systems  that  have  high  loading  capacity  and  can release  their  intrinsic  metal  ions  to  act  as  bioactive  agents.  In  light  of  this,  a  receptor  for  advanced  glycation  end  products (RAGE)  inhibitor,  4-chloro-N-cyclohexyl-N-(phenylmethyl)-benzamide  (FPS-ZM1),  was  loaded  into  a  cobalt  (Co)-based  MOF (zeolitic imidazolate framework-67, ZIF-67) to fabricate FPS-ZM1 encapsulated ZIF-67 (FZ@ZIF-67) nanoparticles (NPs). As a result, FZ@ZIF-67 NPs could dually deliver Co ions and FPS-ZM1 in a controlled manner for over 14 days. Our in vitro study showed that FZ@ZIF-67 NPs not only enhanced angiogenesis by delivering Co ions but also released FPS-ZM1 to promote M2 macrophage polarization and attenuated high glucose (HG)- and/or inflammation-induced impairment of angiogenesis through RAGE  inhibition.  Moreover,  in  an  in  vivo  study,  FZ $@$ ZIF-67  NPs  markedly  improved  re-epithelialization,  collagen  deposition, neovascularization, and relieved inflammation in diabetic wounds in rats. This study not only provides a low-cost, effective, and synergistic proangiogenic bioactive agent but also demonstrates that targeting diabetes-related pathological signaling pathways is necessary to ameliorate vascularization impairment during diabetic wound healing.
## KEYWORDS
zeolitic imidazolate framework-67, drug delivery, dual-controlled release, angiogenesis, diabetic wound
## Introduction
Nonhealing wounds, a common, complex, and costly complication of diabetes that mainly affects the lower extremities, have become a growing health problem, with a lifetime incidence ranging from $15\%$ to $25\%$ in diabetic patients [1]. In particular, these refractory wounds are responsible for an increased risk for limb amputation and impose an annual cost burden of more than $\$25$ billion  on  society  [2, 3].  Under  chronic  hyperglycemic conditions, angiogenesis (the formation of new blood vessels) is significantly  impaired,  and  the  supply  of  oxygen,  nutrients,  and reparative  growth  factors  to  cells  by  blood  vessels  is  obstructed; this latter effect is the main mechanism underlying wound healing failure  [4].  Thus,  the  goal  of  strategies  designed  to  attenuate delayed diabetic wound healing is to restore vascularization in the wound  area  [5].  Although  the  effect  of  conventional proangiogenic  strategies  that  directly  enhance  the  function  of endothelial  cells  (ECs)  through  the  use  of  exogenous  stem  cells, growth  factors,  and/or  gene  therapy  techniques  has  been recognized,  the  safety  and  cost  of  these  methods  are  major obstacles  [5–7].  Therefore,  novel  and  targeted  therapeutic strategies  for  enhancing  angiogenesis  are  urgently  needed  to improve diabetic wound healing.
The  roles  of  appropriate  dynamic  changes  in  the  phenotypes and functions of macrophages in the wound healing process are being  increasingly  emphasized  [8, 9].  In  the  early  stage  of physiological wound healing, macrophages are mainly polarized to the M1 phenotype and mediate proinflammatory actions. As the dynamic  healing  process  progresses,  the  number  of  M2-like macrophages, which prevent excess inflammation and contribute to  revascularization  as  well  as  collagen  deposition  during  tissue regeneration,  gradually  increases  [10].  However,  the  transition from the M1 phenotype to the M2 phenotype is impaired under diabetic conditions, making the wound healing process aberrantly stagnate  in  the  early  proinflammatory  stage  [11–14].  Notably,  a prolonged  and  persistent  inflammatory  microenvironment disrupts  the  functions  of  ECs,  leading  to  impaired  angiogenesis [15, 16].  Receptor  for  advanced  glycation  end  products  (RAGE) signaling pathway plays an important role in the abovementioned diabetes-induced  inflammatory  process  [17, 18].  Activation  of RAGE  triggers  a  series  of  downstream  signaling  pathways associated with inflammation (e.g., nuclear factor kappa B, NF- $\kappa\mathrm{B}$ pathways) and oxidative stress (e.g., NADPH oxidase, Nox), which promote  M1  polarization  and  reduce  the  transition  of macrophages to the M2 phenotype[19–21]. Moreover, enhanced inflammation  and  oxidative  stress  mediated  by  RAGE  signaling can  impair  the  functions  of  ECs;  thus,  RAGE  is  also  directly implicated in the exacerbation of impaired vessel formation [22].
Therefore,  targeting  RAGE  signaling  is  a  promising  strategy  for improving  vascularization  through  attenuation  of  macrophage polarization  dysfunction  rather  than  direct  promotion  of angiogenesis.
The use of an appropriate delivery approach can overcome the limitations of direct application of therapeutic agents, by not only stabilizing  the  small  molecules,  but  also  allowing  persistent  and controlled  release  of  the  drug  [23].  Metal–organic  frameworks (MOFs),  which  are  promising  novel  hybrid  porous  materials comprising  metal  ions  and  organic  bridging  ligands,  have attracted significant research interest in recent years [24]. MOFs possess  great  potential  for  use  as  drug  delivery  systems,  due  to their  various  advantages,  including  a  large  surface  area,  tunable structure,  intrinsic  biodegradability  and  low  toxicity  [25, 26]. Moreover,  drug  encapsulation  and  MOF  synthesis  can  be achieved via a one-step process [27, 28]. The reaction parameters, including  temperature,  pressure,  and $\mathrm{\pH{_{\cdot}}}$ ,  required  for  MOF synthesis are generally mild [29], allowing the molecules to retain their  maximal  bioactivity.  During  the  degradation  of  MOFs, intrinsic  metal  ions  in  combination  with  cargo  can  be released [25, 26]. Interestingly, some metal ions are also essential micronutrients  for  organisms  and  participate  in  a  variety  of physiological processes as cofactors [30]; thus, MOFs can deliver drugs in a controlled manner, and their bioactive metal ions can be  utilized,  making  them  a “functional” drug  delivery  system. Cobalt $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{Co})$ ions  can  mimic  hypoxia  by  stabilizing  hypoxiainducible factor-1 alpha (HIF-1α), and exhibit great potential for promoting  angiogenesis  [31, 32].  Co  is  widely  used  as  a supplement  in  biomaterials  to  encourage  vascularization  [33]. Hence,  a  Co-based  MOF  (zeolitic  imidazolate  framework-67, ZIF-67)  consisting  of  Co  ions  tetrahedrally  bridged  by 2-methylimidazole  (2-mIm)  ligands  attracted  our  attention  [34]. ZIF-67  can  not  only  carry  small  molecules  that  target  diabetesrelated  signaling  pathways  to  attenuate  diabetes-induced impairment of vascularization but also release Co ions to directly promote  angiogenesis.  Therefore,  ZIF-67  may  be  a  promising candidate  functional  drug  delivery  system  for  diabetic  wound healing.
In the present study, 4-chloro-N-cyclohexyl-N-(phenylmethyl)- benzamide (FPS-ZM1), a potent and specific inhibitor of RAGE, was  introduced  into  the  ZIF-67  framework  to  fabricate  a FPS-ZM1-encapsulated  ZIF-67  (FZ@ZIF-67)  nanoplatform  to improve  diabetic  wound  healing.  We  showed  that  the  obtained FZ@ZIF-67 nanoparticles (NPs) could release FPS-ZM1 and Co ions in a controlled manner for over 14 days. Our in vitro results showed that Co ions enhanced angiogenesis directly via activation of HIF-1α and that FPS-ZM1 reduced M1 macrophage-mediated inflammation,  promoted  M2  macrophage  polarization  and attenuated high glucose (HG)- and/or inflammation-induced EC dysfunction (Scheme 1). Furthermore, in a rat model of diabetic wound  healing,  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  markedly  enhanced  wound closure,  re-epithelialization,  collagen  deposition,  vascularization, and  the  transition  of  macrophages  to  the  M2  phenotype.  This work  indicates  the  strong  potential  of  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  as  an effective  and  dual  drug  delivery  system  for  alleviating  the impairment of angiogenesis in diabetic wound healing.
## 2    Experimental
## 2.1    Materials
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate was purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Company (Shanghai, China). 2-mIm was purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Company (Shanghai, China). FPS-ZM1  was  purchased  from  MedChemExpress  (MCE,  NJ, USA)  and  gelatin  methacrylate  (GelMA)  was  obtained  from Sigma-Aldrich (MO, USA).
## 2.2    Synthesis of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs
ZIF-67 was synthesized based on previously published protocols with some modifications [27, 35, 36]. In detail, an FPS-ZM1 stock solution $(5~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ was prepared in methanol. $0.45\mathrm{~g~}$ of cobalt nitrate  hexahydrate  was  dissolved  in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol  and  then added with $2\mathrm{mL}$ of the FPS-ZM1 stock solution. The mixture was stirred for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , and $20~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol containing $5.5\mathrm{~g~}2\mathrm{-mIm}$ was added dropwise. The solution was stirred again for $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. The product was collected by centrifugation, and  the  purple  precipitates  were  washed  successively  with deionized (DI) water and methanol, and finally vacuum-dried for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ FPS-ZM1-free ZIF-67 was synthesized in a similar way by mixing the linker solution and cobalt nitrate hexahydrate solution without the FPS-ZM1 stock solution.
## 2.3    Characterization of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs
The  morphology  and  structure  of  the  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  were evaluated using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, Sirion 200, FEI company, OR, USA). The Co and chlorine contents  were  further  determined  by  surface  scanning  using energy  dispersive  spectrometry  (EDS)  on  the  SEM.  The  crystal structure of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs was evaluated using $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) patterns on a Rigaku Ultima IV X-ray diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=\mathrm{\bar{1}}.5405\mathrm{\:\mathring{A}},35\mathrm{\:kV},$ ,  and $25\mathrm{\mA}$ ).  The  size distribution  and  zeta  potential  of  the  NPs  were  measured  by  a dynamic  light  scattering  analyzer  (Beckman  Coulter,  Delsamax Pro, CA, USA) at room temperature. Thermogravimetric analysis of the NPs was assessed by a thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA, STA 449 F3, Germany) from 25 to $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a constant heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere.
## 2.4    Release  of  FPS-ZM1  and  Co  ions  from  FZ@ZIF-67 NPs
To  evaluate  the  FPS-ZM1  and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ ion  release  profile  of  the FZ@ZIF-67, $5\mathrm{mg}$ of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs were dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of Dulbecco’s  phosphate-buffered  saline  (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}7.4,5.0\$ ,  or  6.5, Gibco) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and continuously shaken at a speed of $100~\mathrm{rpm}$ At  certain  time  intervals, $5\ \mathrm{mL}$ of  the  dialysis  solution  was collected,  and  the  same  volume  of  fresh  PBS  was  added.  The concentration of FPS-ZM1 was measured using high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, Agilent Technologies 1200 series, CA,  USA)  with  ultraviolet  (UV)  detection  at $200~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ .  The concentration of Co ions was measured using a inductive coupled plasma  atomic  emission  spectrometry  (ICP-AES,  iCAP7600, Thermo).  The  crystal  structure  of  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  before  and after incubation was evaluated using XRD patterns. The results are presented  as  the  cumulative  concentration  of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ ions  released over time.
2.5    Culture  of  human  umbilical  vein  endothelial  cells (HUVECs), L929, and RAW 264.7 cells
Primary  HUVECs,  RAW  264.7  (monocyte  cell  lines),  and  L929 (fibroblast  cell  lines)  were  purchased  from  ScienCell  Research Laboratories  (CA,  USA).  HUVECs  were  cultured  in  endothelial culture medium (ECM, $5.5~\mathrm{mM}$ glucose, ScienCell), while RAW 264.7  and  L929  cells  were  cultured  in  HG  Dulbecco’s  modified Eagle’s medium (DMEM; HyClone, PA, USA) according to the manufacturer’s  instructions.  Both  cell  types  were  cultured  in special medium supplemented with $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS; Gibco, NY, USA) and $1\%$ penicillin/streptomycin (Gibco) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
## 2.6    Cell viability assay
The effect of the FZ@ZIF-67 NPs on cell viability was assessed by the Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8) assay (Dojindo, Kyushu Island, Japan)  according  to  the  manufacturer’s  instructions.  To  fully investigate the cytotoxicity of FZ@ZIF-67, HUVECs, RAW 264.7 cells, and L929 cells were used. Briefly, the cells were seeded into 96-well plates at a density of 5,000 cells/well. After attachment, the cells were treated with different concentrations of ZIF-67 NPs or FZ@ZIF-67 NPs. Untreated cells cultured in ECM served as the control group. The CCK-8 assay was performed after treatment for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ ,  and  a  microplate  (Thermo,  MA,  USA)  was  used  to measure the absorbance at $450\mathrm{nm}$ .
Moreover, hemolysis evaluation was also performed according to  the  protocols  reported  in  the  previous  study  [37].  Briefly, human blood from a healthy volunteer was collected and added with sodium citrate $(3.8~\mathrm{wt.\%})$ and diluted with normal saline at volume ratio of 4:5. Then, $0.2~\mathrm{mL}$ diluted blood was added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ normal  saline  that  contained  different  concentrations  of ZIF-67 NPs or FZ@ZIF-67 NPs and incubated for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Blank normal saline was used as negative control while DI water was used as positive control. After centrifugation, the supernatant was  transferred  to  a  96-well  plate,  and  the  optical  density  (OD) value was measured at $545\mathrm{nm}$ . The hemolysis ratio was calculated using Eq. (1)
where $\mathrm{OD}_{\mathrm{t}}$ is the OD value of test group, and $\mathrm{{OD}_{\mathrm{{nc}}}}$ and $\mathrm{{OD_{pc}}}$ are the OD value of negative control and positive control, respectively.
## 2.7    In vitro angiogenic activity
The horizonal and vertical migration of HUVECs were measured by the scratch wound and Transwell assays, respectively, according to  the  methods  described  in  our  previous  study  [38].  For  the scratch  wound  assay, $5\times10^{5}$ HUVECs  were  seeded  into  6-well plates, after the cells reached $100\%$ confluence, a $200\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ pipette tip was used to create a scratch wound and the cells were cultured in serum-free medium added with different agents $\mathrm{\nabla{\cdot}0\mu g/m L}$ ZIF-67 NPs or FZ@ZIF-67 NPs). A light microscope (Leica) was used to monitor  the  closure  of  the  scratch  wounds  at  the  desired  time intervals. The migration rate was calculated using Eq. (2)
$$
\mathrm{Cellmigration~(\%)}=(A_{\mathrm{0}}-A_{\mathrm{r}})/A_{\mathrm{0}}\times100~(\%)
$$
where $A_{0}$ is the area of the original scratch wound at zero hours, and $A_{\mathrm{r}}$ is the area of the scratch wound at the observation time point.
For the Transwell assay, $1\times10^{5}$ HUVECs were suspended in serum-free cell medium and seeded into the upper chambers of 6- well Transwell plates, while complete growth medium containing different agents was added to the lower chamber. After $24\ \mathrm{h},$ the migrated cells on the bottom of the membrane were stained with $1\%$ crystal violet (Thermo) for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The number of migrating cells in five randomly selected fields was calculated using a light microscope.
The  tube  formation  of  HUVECs  was  evaluated  following previously described protocols [38]. Briefly, $8\times10^{4}$ HUVECs were seeded in a 24-well plate coated with Matrigel (Thermo). At the desired time intervals, the cells were incubated with calcein-AM (Thermo)  for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ according  to  the  manufacturer’s instructions,  and  fluorescence  images  were  captured  with  a fluorescence microscope (D-35578, Leica, Wetzlar, Germany).
For HG treatment, the glucose concentration in the ECM was adjusted to $25\mathrm{mM}$ using D-glucose (Sigma-Aldrich) to obtain HGECM. HUVECs were cultured in HG-ECM for over two weeks and then harvested, and angiogenic activity under HG conditions was  evaluated.  Macrophage-conditioned  medium  (CM)  was collected as described in the following experiments.
## 2.8    In vitro macrophage polarization
The polarization of RAW 264.7 cells was induced as described in a previous study [39]. Briefly, RAW 264.7 cells were seeded in 24- well or 6-well plates at a density of $5\times10^{4}\mathrm{cells}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . To induce M1 phenotype polarization, the cells were treated with LPS $\mathrm{(10~ng/mL,~}$ Sigma)  and $\mathrm{IFN-}\gamma$ $(20\mathrm{\ng/mL},$ PeproTech,  NJ,  USA)  with  or without $50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ ZIF-67 NPs or FZ@ZIF-67 NPs for $24\mathrm{h}.$ . The culture medium of the M1-polarized macrophages was collected as  CM  for  further  evaluation.  To  induce  the  transition  of macrophages from M1 phenotype to M2 phenotype, the cells were treated with the M1-induction medium for ${12\mathrm{h}},$ and then treated with  IL-4 $20\mathrm{\ng/mL}$ ,  PeproTech)  and  IL-13 $(20\ \mathrm{\ng/mL},$ PeproTech) for another $12\mathrm{{h}}$ .
The polarization of RAW 264.7 cells was assessed by evaluating the expression of different surface markers using flow cytometry. Briefly,  after  the  cells  were  polarized  using  different  induction media,  they  were  dissociated,  permeabilized  and  then  incubated with  Alexa  Fluor  594-conjugated  iNOS  (1:200,  BD  Biosciences, NY,  USA)  and  Alexa  Fluor  488-conjugated  Arg-1  (1:200,  BD Biosciences)  antibodies  for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ at  room  temperature.  Flow cytometry analysis was performed on a Guava easyCyte™ Systems (Luminex, TX, USA).
The secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines, including TNF- $\mathtt{a}$ and IL-6 was measured by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA,  Neobioscience,  Shenzhen,  China).  After  treatment  with M1  induction  medium  treatment  for $^{24\ \mathrm{h},}$ the  cell  supernatant was  collected  and  ELISA  was  performed  according  to  the manufacturer’s instructions. Intracellular reactive oxygen species (ROS)  levels  were  estimated  using  an  oxidation-sensitive fluorescent probe (DCFH-DA) from the Reactive Oxygen Species Assay Kit (Beyotime, Shanghai, China). Briefly, treatment with M1 induction  medium  treatment  for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ ,  the  cells  were  incubated with DCFH-DA for $30~\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ After dissociation, the mean fluorescence  intensity  was  measured  at  Ex $488/\mathrm{Em}\ 525\$ using  a flow cytometry analyzer.
