## 2.10    Immunofluorescence (IF)
After treatment, cells were fixed using $4\%$ paraformaldehyde, and frozen sections were treated with antigen retrieval solution. After permeabilization with $0.5\%$ Triton $\mathrm{X}{-}100$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ , the cells and sections were blocked with $5\%$ bovine serum albumin (BSA) for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ and then incubated with specific antibodies (Table S2 in the ESM) at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Subsequently, the cells and sections were incubated  with  specific  Alexa  Fluor  488-conjugated  and  Alexa Fluor  594-conjugated  secondary  antibodies  (1:200,  Jackson ImmunoResearch,  PA,  USA)  at  room  temperature  for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .  The nuclei  were  stained  using  DAPI  (Beyotime).  Fluorescence  was detected by fluorescence microscopy.
## 2.11    In vivo rat diabetic wound model
All rats handling and procedures were approved by the Animal Research Committee of Shanghai Jiao Tong University Affiliated Sixth People’s Hospital. According to the protocols described in our  previous  study  [40],  4-week-old  male  Sprague-Dawley  rats received  intraperitoneal  injections  of  streptozotocin  (SigmaAldrich, $60\mathrm{\mg/kg)}$ to  induce  type  1  diabetes  mellitus.  After establishment of the diabetic rat model for 4 weeks, the diabetic rats  were  prepared  for  surgery.  Rats  with  a  blood  glucose concentration  higher  than $\mathrm{16.7~mmol/L}$ were  anesthetized,  and then a standardized full-thickness wound (diameter, $20~\mathrm{mm}.$ ) was made on the dorsum of the diabetic rats. Afterward, the wounds were fixed with a titanium alloy ring (external diameter, $30~\mathrm{mm};$ internal  diameter, $20~\mathrm{mm}$ )  with  4–0  sutures  to  prevent  wound contraction. GelMA was prepared at a concentration of $10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ and  with $0.05\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Irgacure  2959  (Sigma)  according  to  the manufacturer’s  instructions.  Then,  forty  rats  were  randomized into three groups: (a) the control group, which was injected with blank GelMA $(n=12^{\cdot}$ ), (b) the group injected with GelMA with $50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ ZIF-67 NPs ${\bf\Psi}(n=14)$ ), and (c) the group injected with GelMA with $50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ FZ@ZIF-67 NPs $\begin{array}{r}{{'}n=14,}\end{array}$ ). After injection, the hydrogels were exposed to UV light for gelation. At 0, 3, 7, 10, and  14  days  after  surgery,  the  wound  healing  process  was monitored  by  capturing  images  of  every  defect.  The  wound closure rate was calculated as Eq. (3)
## Wound closure rate $(\%)=\left(A_{\mathrm{0}}-A_{\mathrm{r}}\right)/A_{\mathrm{0}}\times100\ (\%)$
where $A_{0}$ is the wound area on day 0, and $A_{\mathrm{r}}$ is the wound area on days 3, 7, 10, and 14. Moreover, at 7 and 14 days after surgery, the rats were anesthetized and placed on a black podium. The blood flow of the wound area was evaluated by a laser Doppler imaging (Perimed,  Jarfalla,  Sweden)  with  the  following  parameters: wavelength of $633~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , exposure time of $30\ s,$ scan area of $4\mathrm{cm}\times$ $4\mathrm{cm},$ , temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
At 7 days after surgery, six rats from each group were randomly sacrificed;  14  days  after  surgery,  the  other  rats  were  sacrificed. Wound  tissue  and  adjacent  normal  skin  were  harvested  for histological analysis.
Vascular perfusion was performed to evaluate neovascularization  during  wound  regeneration  according  to previous studies [41]. Briefly, the ribcages of the rats were opened under  anesthesia  at  14  days  after  surgery,  and  then  heparinized saline, $4\%$ paraformaldehyde, and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of Microfil (Flow Tech, MA, USA) were sequentially perfused into the left ventricle. After the tissues were stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight, the wound tissue was harvested, and assessed by micro-CT to evaluate angiogenesis.
## 2.12    Micro-CT analysis
Wound tissue that had undergone vessel perfusion was scanned using  a  micro-CT  scanner  (Skyscan  1172,  Bruker  micro-CT, Kontich, Belgium) with the following parameters: X-ray energy of $80\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV}}_{\mathrm{P}}$ current of $112\upmu\mathrm{A};$ and exposure time of $370~\mathrm{{ms}}$ . Threedimensional  (3D)  reconstruction  of  the  blood  vessels  was performed, and quantitative parameters, including the blood area and blood vessel number, of the wound area were measured.
## 2.13    Histological examination
After  dehydration,  the  wound  tissue  was  embedded  in  paraffin and  sectioned  at  a  thickness  of $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ through  the  center  area. Hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) and Masson’s trichrome staining were  performed  as  described  previously  [42].  The  wound  reepithelialization rate was calculated by Eq. (4)
Re-epithelialization rate $(\%)=\left(L_{0}-L_{\mathrm{r}}\right)/L_{0}\times100\ (\%)$
where $L_{0}$ indicates the original wound length, and $L_{\mathrm{r}}$ indicates the length  of  the  remaining  wound.  Collagen  was  stained  blue  by Masson’s  trichrome  staining,  and  collagen  deposition  was evaluated by measuring the intensity of the blue areas.
For immunohistochemical staining, we followed the protocols described in our previous study. Briefly, sections were incubated overnight  at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with  a  primary  antibody  specific  to  RAGE (1:200, Abcam). Then, the sections were incubated with specific secondary  antibodies  for  1  h  at  room  temperature.  A diaminobenzidine  color  reaction  kit  was  used  following hematoxylin staining.
IF  staining  was  performed  to  evaluate  angiogenesis  and macrophage polarization as described previously.
## 2.14    Statistical analysis
GraphPad Prism software (GraphPad, CA, USA) was utilized for statistical analysis. The data are displayed as the mean $\pm$ standard
deviation  (SD).  Data  were  compared  by  one-way  analysis  of variance  (ANOVA)  followed  by  Tukey’s  post  hoc  test  among groups. $P<0.05$ was defined as statistically significant.
## 3    Results and discussion
## 3.1    Synthesis and characterization of ZIF-67 and FZ@ZIF67 NPs
FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  were  fabricated  through  a  simple  one-pot synthesis  process  during  the  crystallization  of  ZIF-67,  as schematically  illustrated  in Fig. 1(a).  SEM  was  performed  to observe the morphologies and sizes of the ZIF-67 and FZ@ZIF-67 NPs.  As  shown  in Fig. 1(b),  the  ZIF-67  and  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs exhibited  similar  sizes  and  typical  rhombic  dodecahedron-like morphologies. Moreover, dynamic light scattering (DLS) analysis showed that the average particle sizes of ZIF-67 and FZ@ZIF-67 were 386.0 and $466.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Fig. 1(c)), and the surface zeta  potentials  were  3.63  and $3.10\ \mathrm{mV}_{:}$ ,  respectively  (Fig. 1(d)). Furthermore,  the  powder  XRD  patterns  results  showed  that  the obtained ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{FZ@ZIF-67~NPs}$ had high crystallinity with sharp characteristic peaks that were consistent with those of the simulated  ZIF-67,  implying  that  the  crystal  structure  of  the FZ@ZIF-67 NPs was similar to that of the ZIF-67 NPs (Fig. 1(e)). Notably,  the  diffraction  peaks  of  the  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  (blue) shifted to lower angles than those of ZIF-67 NPs (red), indicating the stretchability of interplanar spacing due to the interposition of the target molecules, which was also consistent with the results of previous studies [35].
The  thermal  characteristics  of  the  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  are displayed  in Fig. 1(f).  As  observed,  the  TG-DSC  patterns  of  the FZ@ZIF-67 NPs showed exothermal peaks that were distinct from those  of  the  ZIF-67  NPs,  indicating  the  encapsulation  of  FPSZM1. The loading amount of FPS-ZM1 was $10.81\%$ , as calculated from the thermogravimetric results. Furthermore, successful FPSZM1 encapsulation was further confirmed by SEM-EDS analysis. As  shown  in Fig. S1  in  the  ESM,  the  elemental  mapping microscopy of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs clearly illustrated that Cl, which was specific to FPS-ZM1 and was not composed in ZIF-67, was homogeneously  distributed  along  with  Co.  The  above  findings implied that FPS-ZM1-loaded ZIF-67 was successfully prepared.
The  pH-sensitive  release  of  FPS-ZM1  from  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs was investigated in aqueous solutions with different pH values. As shown in Fig. 1(g), FPS-ZM1 was gradually released from FZ@ZIF$67\mathrm{NPs}$ in PBS solutions $\mathrm{(pH=7.4)}$ ) instead of undergoing burst release. Only $52.03\%$ of FPS-ZM1 had been released at 14 days, indicating  that  the  encapsulation  of  FPS-ZM1  into  ZIF-67 framework  was  able  to  retained  the  FPS-ZM1  for  over  14  days, which means that FZ@ZIF-67 could provide sustained release of FPS-ZM1  almost  throughout  the  whole  wound  healing  process. Additionally, FPS-ZM1 release was accelerated in acidic solutions, especially in solutions with a $\mathrm{\pH}$ of 5.0, as $83.24\%$ of FPS-ZM1 was released during the first $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. $1(\mathrm{h})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ), possibly due to the enhanced  degradation  of  the  ZIF-67  framework  in  an  acidic environment.  Furthermore,  the  powder  XRD  patterns  also demonstrated the enhanced degradation of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs in an acidic  environment  (Fig. S2  in  the  ESM).  Then,  the  cumulative release of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ ions was measured. As shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM, Co  ions  were  also  gradually  released  in  combination  with  FPSZM1  in  a  similar  manner,  implying  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs possessed a strong ability to dual release of FPS-ZM1 and Co ions. In  fact,  the  microenvironment  became  acidic  in  the  early hematoma  and  inflammation  phases  of  the  wound  healing process,  promoting  the  release  of  FPS-ZM1,  while  the microenvironment  was  restored  to  physiological  conditions $(7.35{-}7.45~\mathrm{\pH})$ in  the  middle-to-late  stage  of  wound  repair, causing  the  sustained  release  of  a  small  amount  of  FPS-ZM1. These findings indicated that ZIF-67 is a promising drug delivery system  and  that  the  release  profile  of  FPS-ZM1  is  desirable  for wound repair.
## 3.2    In vitro biocompatibility of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs
The  biocompatibility  of  biomaterials  is  the  most  important property in terms of clinical use. Wound healing is a complicated dynamic  process  that  involves  various  reparative  cells,  including macrophages  (inflammation),  fibroblasts  (collagen  deposition), and ECs (blood vessel formation) [4, 10]. Thus, RAW 264.7 cells, L929 cells, and HUVECs were used to fully investigate the in vitro cytotoxicity of the FZ@ZIF-67 NPs. The cells were treated with 25, 50, and $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ FZ@ZIF-67 NPs or ZIF-67 NPs for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . As shown  in Fig. 2(a),  the  viability  of  HUVECs  treated  with  25  or $50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ FZ@ZIF-67 NPs or ZIF-67 NPs was greater than $85\%$ . Consistently,  the  viability  of  L929  cells  treated  with  25  or $50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ FZ@ZIF-67 NPs or ZIF-67 NPs was higher than $80\%$ (Fig. 2(b)).  When  the  concentration  of  NPs  was  increased  to $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ cell viability decreased to between $70.25\%$ and $76.09\%$ which is higher than $70\%$ and not considered a cytotoxicity effect according to the standard ISO 10993-5:2009(E) ( $3^{\mathrm{rd}}$ edition) [43]. Moreover, neither ZIF-67 NPs nor FZ@ZIF-67 NPs affected the viability of RAW 264.7 cells at these concentrations (Fig. 2(c)). In addition, hemolysis of the FZ@ZIF-67 NPs was also evaluated. As shown  in Fig. S4  in  the  ESM,  the  hemolysis  ratio  was  slightly increased  as  the  ZIF-67  or  FZ@ZIF-67  NP  concentration increases, but was still lower than $5\%$ , implying that FZ@ZIF-67 did  not  induce  acute  hemolysis.  Therefore,  the  above  findings indicated  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  have  acceptable  biocompatibility in vitro.
## 3.3    Direct pro-angiogenic activities of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs
The  formation  of  new  blood  vessels  plays  a  key  role  in  the appropriate  wound  healing  process  [5, 44].  Angiogenesis  is strongly  determined  by  various  functions  of  ECs,  including migration and tube formation [45]. To evaluate the direct effect of FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  on  HUVECs,  migration  and  tube  formation activities were assessed under normal glucose conditions. First, we evaluated  the  vertical  and  horizonal  migration  of  HUVECs  by Transwell  and  scratch  wound  closure  assays,  respectively.  The results  of  the  scratch  wound  assay  showed  that  the  horizonal migration of HUVECs was significantly enhanced in the ZIF-67 and  FZ@ZIF-67  group,  compared  with  the  control  group (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). Consistently, in the Transwell assay, nearly twice the number of migrating cells was observed in the ZIF-67 or FZ@ZIF-67  treatment  groups  as  in  the  untreated  control  group (Figs. 3(c) and 3(d)). These findings were in agreement with the results  of  the  tube  formation  assay.  The  number  of  tube-like structures was significantly increased after treatment with ZIF-67 NPs or FZ@ZIF-67 NPs, and these tube-like structures were more mature  and  had  better  integrity  in  the  ZIF-67  and  FZ@ZIF-67 groups than in the control group, as shown in Figs. 3(e) and 3(f). However,  neither  the  migration  activity  nor  the  tube  formation activity  of  the  HUVECs  was  significantly  different  between  the ZIF-67  group  and  the  FZ@ZIF-67  group,  indicating  that  FPSZM1,  an  inhibitor  of  RAGE,  had  no  impact  on  the  angiogenic activities  of  HUVECs  under  normal  glucose  conditions.  The above  results  indicated  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  can  promote angiogenesis  of  HUVECs,  mainly  due  to  the  ZIF-67  delivery system itself exhibited a direct proangiogenic effect on HUVECs under normal glucose conditions.
To  further  investigate  the  molecular  mechanisms  underlying the  effect  of  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  on  the  angiogenic  activity  of HUVECs, we assessed the expression level of angiogenesis-related proteins,  including  VE-Cadherin  (important  for  EC  adhesion) [46],  VEGF  (a  key  growth  factor  that  drives  angiogenesis)  [47], and  SDF-1  (a  homeostatic  chemokine  that  recruits  progenitor cells)  [48].  Not  surprisingly,  the  WB  results  revealed  that  the expression  levels  of  all  these  angiogenesis-related  proteins  were significantly enhanced by $20\%$ to $78\%$ after ZIF-67 or FZ@ZIF-67 treatment, compared with the control group. Consistent with the results  of  angiogenesis  activity  assay,  no  obvious  differences  in angiogenic protein levels were observed between the ZIF-67 and FZ@ZIF-67 groups (Figs. $3(\mathrm{g)}$ and $3(\mathrm{h})$ ). At the molecular level, the  ZIF-67  delivery  system  promoted  the  expression  of  these angiogenic proteins; however, FPS-ZM1 had no direct impact on their  function.  In  addition,  cobalt  ions  can  mimic  hypoxia  and trigger  the  HIF-1α  signaling  pathway  to  promote  angiogenesis [31]. Hence, to further confirm whether the proangiogenic effect of  ZIF-67  was  associated  with  HIF-1α,  the  expression  levels  of HIF-1α  and  molecules  associated  with  two  related  regulatory signaling  pathways,  Erk1/2  and  Akt,  in  HUVECs  were  also assessed. As expected, the HIF-1α level was significantly increased after both ZIF-67 and FZ@ZIF-67 treatment, possibly in a manner mediated by Co ion release from ZIF-67 NPs (Figs. $3(\mathrm{g)}$ and $3(\mathrm{h})\dot{}$ ). Moreover,  the  phosphorylation  levels  of  Erk1/2  and  Akt  were enhanced  in  the  ZIF-67  and  FZ@ZIF-67  groups  (Figs.  3(i) and 3(j)),  indicating  that  the  Erk1/2  and  Akt  pathways,  where  are upstream angiogenesis-related signaling pathways, were activated. In summary, these results implied that the ZIF-67 delivery system itself exerts desirable proangiogenic effects through the controlled release  of  Co  ions,  which  can  promote  the  migration  and  tube formation  of  HUVECs  by  activating  HIF-1α  and  angiogenesisrelated signaling pathways (Fig. $3(\mathrm{k}))$ ).
## 3.4    FZ@ZIF-67  NP-mediated M2-like macrophage polarization suppresses inflammation
Persistent and excessive inflammation and impaired transition of macrophages  to  the  M2  phenotype  have  been  increasingly recognized as some of the main mechanisms underlying wound healing  failure  under  diabetic  conditions  [10, 11].  Thus,  we postulated  that  the  application  of  FPS-ZM1,  a  RAGE  inhibitor, encapsulated  in  ZIF-67  NPs  would  reduce  M1  polarization  and promote  the  M2  polarization  of  macrophages  to  alleviate  the diabetes-induced impairment of angiogenesis. Herein, the effect of FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  on  macrophage  polarization  was  further evaluated in the RAW 264.7 cell line, a widely used monocyte cell line. IF staining of iNOS (a marker of M1-like macrophages) and Arg-1 (a marker of M2-like macrophages) was performed, and the results are shown in Fig. 4(a). There was no significant difference in  the  number  of  iNOS  (M1)-  or  Arg-1  (M2)-positive  cells between  the  ZIF-67  NP-treated  groups  and  the  control  group, meaning that ZIF-67 NPs may not affect macrophage polarization. Notably,  when  cells  were  polarized  to  the  M1  phenotype,  the number of iNOS-positive macrophages was markedly reduced in the  FZ@ZIF-67  group,  compared  with  the  control  and  ZIF-67 group,  indicating  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  can  inhibit  the  M1 polarization of macrophages. Moreover, more M2-like macrophages, which highly expressed Arg-1, were observed after FZ@ZIF-67 NP treatment. Furthermore, the effect of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs on polarization was confirmed by flow cytometry (Fig. 4(b)). Consistent  with  the  IF  results,  flow  cytometry  revealed  a significant decrease in the ratio of iNOS-positive cells (Fig. 4(c)) and  a  marked  increase  in  the  ratio  of  Arg-1-positive  cells (Fig. 4(d))  in  the  FZ@ZIF-67  group,  compared  with  the  control and  the  ZIF-67  group.  Taken  together,  these  findings preliminarily  suggested  that  FZ@ZIF-67  can  suppress  the  M1 (proinflammatory) polarization and enhance the M2 (reparative) polarization  of  macrophages,  possibly  through  the  RAGE inhibitor FPS-ZM1.
In diabetic nonhealing wounds, pathological activation of M1 macrophages  can  enhance  inflammation  and  oxidative  stress, leading to an inflammatory microenvironment and exacerbating local  cell  dysfunction  [49].  As  we  found  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs promote the switch from the M1 phenotype to the M2 phenotype, the effect of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs on inflammation and oxidative stress was  further  evaluated.  The  secretion  of  proinflammatory cytokines, including TNF- $\cdot\mathtt{a}$ and IL-6, was measured by ELISA. As shown  in Figs.  4(e) and 4(f),  the  levels  of  proinflammatory mediators were significantly enhanced in the groups treated with M1  induction  medium  compared  with  the  group  treated  with normal  medium  (M0).  Our  findings  also  indicated  that  the activation of M1-like macrophages led to increased inflammation. The  levels  of  inflammatory  cytokines  were  reduced  in  the FZ@ZIF-67  NP-treated  groups  compared  with  the  control  and ZIF-67  NP-treated  groups,  implying  that  M1  macrophageinduced  inflammation  was  relieved  by  FZ@ZIF-67  NP administration.  In  addition,  oxidative  stress  was  evaluated  by measuring  the  generation  of  ROS.  Intracellular  ROS  levels  were estimated with the fluorescence of DCFH-DA on flow cytometry, and ROS levels in macrophages were significantly enhanced after M1  polarization.  Consistently,  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  suppressed  the production  of  ROS  (Fig. $4(\mathrm{g),}$ ).  These  findings  further  indicated that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  can  not  only  modulate  the  phenotypic transition  of  macrophages  but  also  attenuate  M1  macrophagemediated inflammation and oxidative stress.
To clarify the molecular mechanism by which FZ@ZIF-67 NPs affect polarization in vitro, the levels of some proteins involved in RAGE  signaling,  specifically  NF- $\kappa\mathrm{B}$ and  Nox4,  which  not  only participate in inflammation and oxidative stress, respectively, but also  acts  as  crucial  regulators  of  M1  polarization  [19–21],  were evaluated.  The  expression  level  of  RAGE  was  significantly enhanced in the M1 polarization groups compared with the M0 (blank)  group  (Figs.  4(h) and 4(i)),  implying  that  RAGE  is activated  during  the  M1  polarization  of  macrophages,  which  is consist with the findings of previous studies [50]. Moreover, the levels of NF- $\kappa\mathrm{B}$ and Nox4, markers of inflammation and oxidative stress,  were  also  enhanced  in  the  M1  polarization  groups.  After FZ@ZIF-67 treatment, the enhanced expression of RAGE in M1- like  macrophages  was  apparently  suppressed.  Moreover,  the protein levels of NF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\kappa\mathbf{B}$ and Nox4 were reduced in the FZ@ZIF-67 NP-treated  groups  compared  to  the  M0  group  (Figs.  4(h) and 4(i)).  At  the  molecular  level,  the  pathological  activation  of  these proinflammatory  mediators  during  the  M1  polarization  process was  markedly  decreased  by  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  via  inhibition  of RAGE.  Our  results  indicated  that  inhibition  of  the  pathological activation  of  RAGE  effectively  reduces  activation  of  the inflammatory process and modulates macrophage polarization.
## 3.5    FZ@ZIF-67 NPs attenuate HG- and/or inflammationmediated impaired angiogenesis in vitro
Under  chronic  hyperglycemia  conditions,  ECs  deteriorate extensively and exhibit reduced angiogenic abilities [4, 51]. Thus, evaluating the proangiogenic effects of bioactive materials under normal glucose conditions alone is insufficient and inappropriate. Thus,  we  also  evaluated  the  effects  of  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  on vascularization  in  HUVECs  exposed  to  HG  conditions in  vitro. Both the horizonal and vertical migration of HUVECs were only slightly enhanced in the $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{ZIF}$ group compared with the HG group, as shown in Figs. 5(a), 5(b), 5(d), and 5(e). Moreover, the tube-like  structure  formation  of  HUVECs  was  not  significantly different  between  the  HG  group  and  the  HG $^+$ ZIF  group (Figs. 5(c) and 5(f)). These results showed that the enhancement of the HUVEC angiogenic activities by ZIF-67 treatment was not markable  under  HG  conditions.  However,  the  horizonal migration,  vertical  migration  and  tube  formation  activities of  HUVECs  were  still  significantly  promoted  in  the $\mathrm{HG~+~}$ $\mathrm{FZ@ZIF}$ group  compared  with  the  HG  and $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{ZIF}$ groups (Figs. 5(a)–5(f)). Our findings indicated that while ZIF-67 had a significant  direct  proangiogenic  effect  under  normal  glucose conditions, this effect was partially reduced under HG conditions. In other words, the function of HUVEC was impaired under HG conditions,  and  HUVECs  exhibited  a  lower  response  to proangiogenic  treatments.  In  fact,  considerable  evidence  has suggested  that  RAGE  plays  a  vital  role  in  HG-induced  cell dysfunction  [22, 40].  Thus,  the  addition  of  FPS-ZM1,  which inhibits  RAGE,  was  able  to  precisely  alleviate  this  dysfunction. Indeed,  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  was  helpful  to  attenuate  HG-induced HUVEC  dysfunction  and  preserved  a  promising  proangiogenic ability under HG conditions.
In  addition  to  HG,  prolonged  inflammation  is  another important  factor  that  contributes  to  EC  dysfunction  in  diabetic nonhealing wounds [10, 11]. Hence, HUVECs were treated with CM  from  M1  macrophages  subjected  to  different  treatments (blank,  ZIF-67  NPs,  and  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs)  combined  with  HG treatment.  As  shown  in Figs.  5(a)–5(f),  reduced  migration  and tube  formation  of  HUVECs  were  observed  in  the $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{CM}$ group  compared  with  the  HG  group.  Moreover,  the  angiogenic activities of HUVECs were not significantly enhanced in the ${\mathrm{HG}}+$ $\mathrm{CM}+\mathrm{ZIF}$ group  compared  with  the $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{CM}$ group.  These findings  showed  that  the  angiogenic  functions  of  HUVECs  are extremely impaired and that the proangiogenic effect of ZIF-67 is also  highly  restricted  under  the  dual  influences  of  HG  and inflammation. However, this impairment of HUVEC angiogenic activity  induced  by  HG  and  inflammation  was  relieved  by
FZ@ZIF-67 treatment, as both migration and tube formation were increased in the $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{CM}+\mathrm{FZ@}\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ groups, compared with the $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{CM}$ and $\mathrm{HG}+\mathrm{CM}+\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67\$ group (Figs. 5(a)–5(f)). As mentioned  above,  we  demonstrated  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  can suppress  M1  macrophage  polarization  and  reduce  the  levels  of inflammatory  cytokines  through  inhibition  of  RAGE.  Thus,  the encapsulation of FPS-ZM1 in ZIF-67 NPs was effective in treating the  negative  impact  of  inflammation  on  EC  functions.  In summary, we exposed HUVECs to HG conditions and CM from M1 macrophages to imitate HG- and inflammation-induced EC dysfunction in vitro. Nevertheless, FZ@ZIF-67 NPs maintained a strong ability to promote angiogenesis under the dual challenge of HG and inflammation.
## 3.6    Effect of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs on diabetic wound healing in vivo
The aforementioned in vitro results systematically indicated that FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  possess  desirable  proangiogenic  effects  even under  HG  conditions  as  well  as  favorable  biocompatibility  and thus  have  promising  therapeutic  potential  for  the  treatment  of diabetic wounds. Herein, a rat diabetic wound model was used to further evaluate the therapeutic effect of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs in vivo. Considering  the  form  of  ZIF-67  and  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  were powder, they were dispersed into GelMA hydrogels to allow local administration.  Full-thickness  skin  wounds  were  created  on diabetic  rats,  and  then  blank  GelMA  and  GelMA  containing ZIF-67  or  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs $(50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ were  injected  into  the wound region and exposed to UV light for photocrosslinking. The wound size was continuously monitored at $0,3,7,10,$ , and 14 days (Fig. 6(a)).  As  shown  in Figs.  6(a) and $6(\mathrm{b})$ ,  at  an  early  stage during wound healing process (3 days), the average wound closure rate of the Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 group reached $43.29\%\pm2.59\%$ , which was significantly higher than that of the Gel-ZIF-67 group $15.07\%$ $\pm\:3.39\%$ ) and the GelMA control group $(15.12\%\pm1.61\%)$ ). This trend was more obvious in the middle stage of the wound healing process (7 days), at which point the average wound closure rate of the  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  group  had  increased  to $78.94\%\pm\:6.11\%$ which was nearly twice that of the GelMA control group $36.58\%$ $\pm6.25\%)$ ) and was also markedly higher than that of the Gel-ZIF67 group $(50.91\%\pm6.70\%)$ ). Moreover, the wounds treated with Gel-FZ $@$ ZIF-67  showed  considerable  closure  at  days  10  to  14. These  results  showed  that  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  strongly  promoted diabetic wound healing, especially in the first 3 days and thus led to accelerated wound closure at an earlier stage. Furthermore, the Gel-ZIF-67  group  showed  better  wound  closure  compared  with the GelMA control group at days 7, 10, and 14; the wound closure rate was increased to $84.86\%\pm5.61\%$ at 14 days, which was higher than that of the GelMA group $(75.90\%\pm9.48\%$ but lower than that of the Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 group $(95.43\%\pm1.92\%)$ ), implying that Gel-ZIF-67 induced better wound repair. However, there were no significant differences in wound closure between the Gel-ZIF-67 group  and  the  GelMA  group  at  day  3,  suggesting  that  the promoting effect of ZIF-67 on wound healing was restricted in the early inflammatory stage.
Furthermore,  histological  analysis  of  the  wound  area  was performed  to  evaluate  the  quality  of  wound  repair  at  the  tissue level by H&E and Masson’s trichrome staining. As displayed in Fig. $6(\mathrm{c})$ ,  the  granulation  tissue  (scar)  was  in  the  center  of  the wound area and was surrounded by neoepidermis. At days 7 and 14, the Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 group showed the shortest scar width and best  neoepidermis  formation,  followed  by  the  Gel-ZIF-67  group and  the  GelMA  control  group  (Figs.  6(c) and $6(\mathrm{d})_{.}^{.}$ ),  which  was consistent  with  the  macroscopic  results  (Fig. 6(a)).  The  H&E staining results also demonstrated that by day 14, the Gel-FZ@ZIF67 group showed continuous and stratified epithelium tissue and clearly  identified  skin  appendages  including  hair  follicles  and sebaceous glands (Fig. S5(a) in the ESM). As shown in the Masson’ s trichrome staining images, collagen deposition was enhanced in the  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  group  compared  with  the  GelMA  control group and Gel-ZIF-67 group (Figs. 6(e) and $6(\mathrm{f),}$ . By day 14, the wounds treated with Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 showed formation of large wavy collagen fibers in the defect area, which were quite similar to those  in  the  surrounding  normal  skin  (Fig. S5(b)  in  the  ESM). These  results  demonstrated  that  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  not  only accelerate diabetic wound healing but also improve the quality of wound  healing  by  promoting  re-epithelialization  and  collagen deposition.
Impaired angiogenesis has been recognized as one of the main mechanisms underlying nonhealing wounds in diabetes, and our in vitro results demonstrated that FZ@ZIF-67 NPs can promote angiogenesis  under  normal  glucose  and  even  HG  and inflammatory conditions. Hence, neovascularization in the wound area was assessed to further evaluate the proangiogenic effect of FZ@ZIF-67  NPs in  vivo. Figures  7(a) and 7(b) show  that  the intensity of blood flow in the Gel-ZIF-67 group was higher than that  in  the  GelMA  control  group  and  that  the  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 group exhibited the highest blood flow intensity on days 7 and 14. In  addition  to  blood  flow,  3D  micro-CT  reconstruction  of  the perfused blood vessels indicated that the Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 had the highest volume of blood vessels in the wound area at day 14 after surgery,  followed  by  the  Gel-ZIF-67  group  and  GelMA  group (Fig. 7(c)).  The  quantitative  data  also  demonstrated  that  the percentage  of  the  vessel  area  in  the  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  group  was significantly increased to $23.68\%\pm4.67\%$ , which was significantly higher than that in the Gel-ZIF-67 group $(14.96\%\pm4.08\%)$ ) and the  GelMA  group $(6.02\%\pm3.54\%)$ ,  as  shown  in Fig. 7(d). Moreover,  a  similar  trend  in  vessel  number  was  observed,  as shown  in Fig. 7(e).  The  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  and  Gel-ZIF-67  groups showed more blood vessels in the wound area than the GelMA group, and consistently, the $\mathrm{Gel-FZ@ZIF-67}$ group exhibited the largest amount of new blood vessel formation. Histologically, the neovascularization  was  evaluated  by  IF  analysis  of  CD31  and $\mathtt{a}$ -SMA.  The  quantitative  results  showed  that  the  formation  of blood vessels was promoted in both the Gel-FZ@ZIF-67 and GelZIF-67  groups  compared  with  the  control  group  (Figs.  7(f) and $7(\mathrm{g))}$ .  Although  the  number  of $\mathrm{CD}31^{+}$ blood  vessels  was  not different  between  the  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  and  Gel-ZIF-67  groups, more  mature  vessels  that  highly  expressed $\mathtt{a}$ -SMA  (green)  were observed  in  the  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  group  (Fig. $7(\mathrm{h})\dot{}$ ).  Our  results implied that FZ@ZIF-67 exerts a marked proangiogenic effect in
vivo.
Moreover,  macrophage  polarization  in  diabetic  wounds  after different treatments was also evaluated by IF analysis of CD86 and CD206.  As  shown  in Fig. S6  in  the  ESM,  M1  polarization,  as indicated by CD86 staining, was significantly reduced in the GelFZ@ZIF-67  group  compared  with  the  GelMA  and  Gel-ZIF-67 groups. Additionally, FZ@ZIF-67 also enhanced M2 macrophage polarization, as indicated by high expression of CD206, compared with the other two groups. Consistent with the in vitro results, our in vivo findings suggested that FZ@ZIF-67 was helpful to suppress the  M1  polarization  and  promote  the  M2  polarization  of macrophages  during  the  diabetic  wound  healing  process. Mechanistically,  the  involvement  of  RAGE  signaling  was  also evaluated.  The  IHC  staining  results  indicated  that  the  RAGE expression level in the wound area was reduced after Gel-FZ $@$ ZIF67 treatment. The quantitative data also showed that the number of  RAGE-positive  cells  was  significantly  decreased  in  the  GelFZ@ZIF-67 group compared with that of the GelMA group and Gel-ZIF-67 group (Fig. S6 in the ESM).
Furthermore, the in vivo biocompatibility of FZ@ZIF-67 NPs were  also  examined.  The  H&E  staining  of  the  major  organs including the heart, liver, lung, kidney and spleen were performed at  14  days  after  surgery.  As  shown  in Fig. S7  in  the  ESM,  the organs showed no histological abnormalities after the Gel-ZIF-67 or  Gel-FZ@ZIF-67  treatments  for  14  days  compared  with  the
GelMA  control  group,  indicating  good in  vivo biosafety  of  the FZ@ZIF-67 NPs.
In summary, the in vivo results indicated that FZ@ZIF-67 NPs can effectively promote neovascularization and improve diabetic wound  healing.  Compared  with  bare  ZIF-67  NPs,  FZ@ZIF-67 NPs  strongly  promoted  the  transition  of  macrophages  from  the M1  to  the  M2  phenotype.  Hence,  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  enhanced blood vessel formation and maturation by alleviating the diabetesinduced  impairment  of  angiogenesis.  In  particular,  the in  vitro results showed that the release of FPS-ZM1 from the FZ@ZIF-67 nanoplatform was accelerated in an acidic environment and that this  drug  release  profile  of  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  was  suitable  for attenuating early-stage inflammation and improving angiogenesis at  an  earlier  time  point.  Indeed,  FZ@ZIF-67  NPs  not  only improved wound healing but also achieved better wound closure during the first several days than bare ZIF-67 NPs. Therefore, in this work, FZ@ZIF-67 NPs exerted promising anti-inflammatory and  proangiogenic  effects  as  well  as  good  biosafety  for  the treatment of diabetic nonhealing wounds.
## 4    Conclusions
In  conclusion,  we  successfully  fabricated  a  multifunctional  FPSZM1-encapsulated  ZIF-67-derived  nanoplatform  as  a  dualcontrolled release system to effectively and synergistically achieve angiogenesis during diabetic wound healing. The encapsulation of
FPS-ZM1 can be achieved during ZIF-67 synthesis by a one-pot method. As a result, the ZIF-67 delivery system exerted a direct proangiogenetic  effect,  and  most  importantly,  the  controlled release of FPS-ZM1 enhanced the transition of macrophage from the  M1  to  the  M2  phenotype  and  relieved  HG  and/or inflammation-induced impairment of neovascularization, leading to a synergistic improvement in angiogenesis. Finally, FZ@ZIF-67 significantly  promoted  re-epithelialization,  collagen  deposition, and  angiogenesis  and  reduced  inflammation  in  rat  diabetic wounds.  Hence,  this  work  suggests  that  the  use  of  FZ@ZIF-67 NPs is a new strategy for synergistically promoting angiogenesis for the treatment of diabetic wounds.
